  "final_text": "All four steps are done in order, and the rule reads back enabled.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`). The time field is `timestamp`. Fields include `@timestamp`, `response` (text, with `response.keyword`), `timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `url`, `agent`, `geo.*`, and others.\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-e96937c4 Web logs'** (id `886b4822-084e-494b-942f-832d74c23e3b`) with exactly two panels, both on the data view by reference:\n   - A line chart of count over `timestamp`.\n   - A pie chart of count by `response.keyword`, with up to 20 terms. I chose a pie because the reference documents that shape for \"by response code\". A bar chart would also fit.\n\n3. **Alert rule 'S4-e96937c4 5xx errors'** (id `660371fe-c029-4195-a4e5-9d879c49a494`), type `.es-query`, KQL search-source on the data view.\n   - Filter: `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`.\n   - Runs every 1m, counts over a 5m window, fires when count > 10, with `timeField` `timestamp`.\n   - Created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n   - The filter is a lexical keyword range. It covers 500\u2013599 exactly as long as every `response` value is a 3-digit code. I could not check the value distribution, because the Elasticsearch and ES|QL endpoints are not reachable from here.\n\n4. **Panel rename**: the response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. The line chart is still 'Requests over time'. I re-read the dashboard to confirm the change. Reading the rule back gave `enabled: True`, with the first execution status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
